---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';

const title = "The New Private Parking Code of Practice: What Changes for Drivers | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "From 21 December 2026, a new government code replaces the industry's Single Code. What it changes for airport drop-off drivers, and what it doesn't.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/new-private-parking-code/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "The new Private Parking Code of Practice: what changes for drivers",
  "description": description,
  "mainEntityOfPage": canonical,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-11-26",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-26"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader
    title="The new Private Parking Code of Practice: what changes for drivers"
    breadcrumbLabel="New Private Parking Code"
    breadcrumbHref="/guides/new-private-parking-code/"
  />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>From 21 December 2026, private parking companies in England, Scotland and Wales will have a new rulebook. It is the Private Parking Code of Practice. The government wrote it. It replaces the industry code that applied before 21 December 2026.</p>
        <p>This guide covers the parts that matter if you get a PCN at an airport drop-off.</p>

        <h2>What the Code is</h2>
        <p>The Code sets the minimum standards operators must follow to issue and enforce parking charges. They must also follow it to request your details from the DVLA.</p>
        <p>It is not an Act of Parliament. It works through other levers. The government must consider whether an operator follows the Code when deciding whether to give it DVLA keeper details. It must also consider this when deciding whether a trade association stays accredited. Independent bodies approved by the United Kingdom Accreditation Service (UKAS) will check that operators comply.</p>
        <p>The Code also says that following it does not prove a charge is fair or lawful in your case. Consumer law still applies.</p>
        <p>Some standards may phase in. The government says it will publish transitional arrangements. Check <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/private-parking-code-of-practice-2026-explanatory-memorandum/private-parking-code-of-practice-2026-explanatory-memorandum" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK</a> for the current position.</p>

        <h2>What gets better for drivers</h2>
        <h3>More time to decide</h3>
        <p>Drivers get at least 5 minutes to decide whether to park, in most car parks. Most also get 10 minutes to leave after their time ends.</p>
        <p>Airport drop-offs are different. They count as short stay. The minimum there is 5 minutes to decide, with no grace period at the end. Under the industry code that applied before 21 December 2026, it was 1 minute.</p>

        <h3>Driving in is not parking</h3>
        <p>The Code says the moment you enter the land must not, without more, be treated as the start of the parking contract (2.22). It also says no charge should be issued where a driver considered the terms and chose not to park (Appeals Charter, 19).</p>

        <h3>Camera evidence has to be checked</h3>
        <p>A charge based on camera images needs a clear picture of your number plate, an accurate time stamp, and a manual check of the images.</p>

        <h3>Keepers are not presumed to be the driver</h3>
        <p>Operators must not presume the keeper was driving when they issue or pursue a charge (8.1.3). Our guides on <a href="/guides/elliott-v-loake-why-it-doesnt-make-you-liable-as-the-keeper/">Elliott v Loake</a> and <a href="/guides/driving-licence-request/">driving licence requests</a> explain why this matters.</p>

        <h3>Notices must be honest about their basis</h3>
        <p>If a notice is not issued under the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012, it must not refer to that Act. Notices must not look like council penalty notices. They cannot use words such as "fine" or "penalty".</p>

        <h3>Better appeals</h3>
        <p>Operators must reply to an appeal within 28 days, or say when they will. Late appeals must be considered in exceptional cases, such as a hospital stay or a family emergency. Enforcement must stop while a second-stage appeal is pending. Operators must accept the appeals service's decision. If you appeal within 28 days, you keep the right to pay the reduced amount for 14 days after a rejection. The notice must also say you lose the reduced amount if you lose at the second stage. See <a href="/guides/what-responses-to-expect/">what to expect after you appeal</a>.</p>

        <h3>An Appeals Charter</h3>
        <p>The Code lists situations where a charge should not be issued, or should be cancelled with evidence. It also sets a £20 charge for honest mistakes, such as forgetting to display a permit.</p>

        <h3>Complaints</h3>
        <p>Operators must acknowledge a complaint within 14 days and give a full reply within 28 days. A complaint that is really an appeal must be treated as an appeal.</p>

        <h3>Staff incentives</h3>
        <p>Operators cannot reward staff for issuing more notices.</p>

        <h3>Data</h3>
        <p>Operators must keep and report data, including charges by type of site. The Code's list includes airports, and whether a site has a barrier.</p>

        <h2>What does not change</h2>
        <p>The maximum charge is still £100. It is £60 if you pay within 14 days. The Code says this cap does not mean a charge will be fair or payable in every case. A debt recovery fee of up to £70 can still be added.</p>
        <p>Operators can still charge at airport drop-offs. We think £100 is too high for a drop-off. That is our opinion.</p>
        <p>The Code does not overrule byelaws. Where byelaws apply, the operator must say whether it is enforcing the byelaw or a contract (Annex D). It must also hold written landowner confirmation covering the byelaws before it issues charges (14.1).</p>

        <h2>Which Code applies to my PCN?</h2>
        <p>The new Code applies from 21 December 2026. For a charge issued before that date, check the transitional arrangements on GOV.UK. If you are unsure, cite both Codes in your appeal. Name the clause each time.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key point:</strong> The new Code gives drivers more time, firmer appeal rights and a ban on presuming the keeper was driving. It does not lower the £100 cap. It is also not a court ruling, so you still have to make your case.
        </div>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>

